    "text": "Dark the learn, let it sink, knuckles wide around the brink, leading twine, marked by mark, find the bottom in the dark. The fish has taught me depth by touch, what not to keep in careful count, no grand advice, no silver face. Just touch in patience, keep in place, I let it slip through thumb and thumb. About the seagull cold and calm, every feather took a thread from things I thought were better dead. There was the island, there the pale, there the shirt that failed to sail, there the mouth that called it neat. While quietly teaching me to bleed, some depth sensor when you ask, some keep wearing their old masks, some return of grain of sand and ice. Like what else your grip is hiding, sound it out, don't drag it up, not every bottom wants a cup, mark the depth and let it be. There are truths beneath the sea, sound it out, then give it slack, something speak by, not coming back. If I must cross what I don't see, let my hands remember me. At 20 marks the water cooled, at 30 I stopped feeling fooled, at 40 something and me knew the old egg had an underside too, it wasn't just the anchors pulled. It wasn't only hunger's hollow, it was the fear of open drift, of having no excuse to lift. The line came back with clay and shells, small proof from where the quiet settled, I held it like a rough command and rinsed the clay from every knuckle it doesn't vanish. When it's named it doesn't owe you any change, but what you measure without pride can stop deciding from inside. Sound it out, don't drag it up, not every bottom wants a cup, mark the depth and let it be. There are truths beneath the sea, sound it out, then give it slack, something speak by, not coming back. If I must cross what I don't see, let my hands remember me. No cure, no ink or no final word, I let that first become absurd, no blessed turn, no clean design, just wet, broke measure through my grip. And if I shake, let me shake, if I learn, let it take mark by mark, not too fast, depth is not averted. Drop the line, let it sink, leave some room around the brink, lead in twine, palm and breath, I can cross without a myth.",
